WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.870
Google Cloud CLI output is often consumed
by later automation.

2
00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:07.893
A script may query JSON, extract a
resource name, inspect IAM policy data,

3
00:00:07.973 --> 00:00:10.898
select a target, compare configuration,
write a file,

4
00:00:10.978 --> 00:00:13.204
or decide whether a later step should run.

5
00:00:13.284 --> 00:00:17.417
That output can include project details,
endpoints, service account names,

6
00:00:17.497 --> 00:00:22.012
storage locations, log results, IAM
bindings, policy data, and sensitive context.

7
00:00:22.092 --> 00:00:25.335
Structured parsing is safer than fragile
text assumptions.

8
00:00:25.415 --> 00:00:29.930
Scripts should parse expected JSON fields
deliberately and handle missing fields,

9
00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:34.144
empty responses, multiple matches,
paginated data, and unexpected values.

10
00:00:34.224 --> 00:00:36.767
A missing result is not automatically
success,

11
00:00:36.847 --> 00:00:40.345
and multiple results may mean the target
was not specific enough.

12
00:00:40.425 --> 00:00:45.067
Later automation should make decisions
from fields that were intentionally selected

13
00:00:45.147 --> 00:00:45.910
and verified.

14
00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:50.442
Output files should go to approved
destinations with appropriate permissions and

15
00:00:50.522 --> 00:00:51.921
retention expectations.

16
00:00:52.001 --> 00:00:55.244
Generated JSON, IAM policy data, service
account listings,

17
00:00:55.324 --> 00:00:57.359
deployment details, inventory data,

18
00:00:57.439 --> 00:01:01.890
and support bundles can reveal sensitive
operational context even when they do not

19
00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:03.051
contain raw tokens.

20
00:01:03.131 --> 00:01:07.010
A script should avoid writing sensitive
output into shared workspaces,

21
00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:10.651
broad CI/CD artifacts, or long-lived logs
without a clear reason.

22
00:01:10.731 --> 00:01:13.211
Logs should provide minimal useful
evidence.

23
00:01:13.291 --> 00:01:17.043
Good logs show high-level identity,
project, region or zone, action,

24
00:01:17.123 --> 00:01:19.031
target category, time, and result.

25
00:01:19.111 --> 00:01:22.291
They avoid tokens, service account key
material, secrets,

26
00:01:22.371 --> 00:01:25.995
sensitive IAM details, private data, and
excessive raw responses.

27
00:01:26.075 --> 00:01:30.654
Secure output handling lets automation
remain useful without turning logs and files

28
00:01:30.734 --> 00:01:32.260
into another exposure path.
